# Financial Intermediation and the Theory of the Firm: An Analysis of Savings and Loan Association Behavior

**Authors:** George G. Daly

## Abstract
diaries and the implications of this behavior for monetary processes and policies. However, due to these theorists' primary concern with aggregative variables, discussions of this phenomenon have generally used macroeconomic or general equilibrium frameworks. As a result, little analytical attention has been paid to the microeconomics of the particular institutional forms taken by these intermediaries or to the allocative functions played by them. The purpose of this paper is to partially rectify the problem described above. A partial equilibrium framework for the analysis of savings and loan associations is established. Within that framework, three alternative behavioral hypotheses are proposed and examined. Finally and briefly, the implications of the models for the behavior of savings and loan markets are discussed. It is hoped that this paper will result in a better conceptualization of the financial linkages provided by savings and loans and similar institutions and of the effects of monetary policy upon these linkages.

